WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.694
Episode thirteen: bring your own case.

2
00:00:02.694 --> 00:00:11.203
It assumes APEX with OPM Flow (episode one), Claude Code as the model (episode twelve), and the web UI (episode eleven).

3
00:00:11.203 --> 00:00:13.259
A language model is optional.

4
00:00:13.259 --> 00:00:20.568
Without one, add --no-llm and the converter decides by its rules.

5
00:00:20.568 --> 00:00:30.865
We convert the deck that ships with APEX, a 60 × 60 × 5 three-phase box with three producers and three water injectors, as a user would bring it, with no model.

6
00:00:30.865 --> 00:00:33.053
It never runs a simulation itself.

7
00:00:33.053 --> 00:00:51.895
It takes under a minute and prints what it chose.

8
00:00:51.895 --> 00:00:53.766
We open my_box/CONVERSION.md.

9
00:00:53.766 --> 00:00:57.379
Every value the converter had to assume is listed there.

10
00:00:57.379 --> 00:01:17.691
The prior is one stationary field per property, with the deck's mean and variance, not the deck's values cell by cell.

11
00:01:17.691 --> 00:01:25.127
In the configuration it wrote: The method is GIES with adaptive localization, 100 members and 10 iterations.

12
00:01:25.127 --> 00:01:42.161
A converted case is real: it has no true model, so its metrics are the data mismatches only.

13
00:01:42.161 --> 00:01:47.883
With a model, say what you want in words, and the converter follows it where the deck allows.

14
00:01:47.883 --> 00:01:51.882
Here: porosity only, with ES-MDA in four steps and fifty members.

15
00:01:51.882 --> 00:02:14.364
The model's choices are checked against the deck.

16
00:02:14.364 --> 00:02:17.886
Before running, we validate the converted case.

17
00:02:17.886 --> 00:02:32.633
It gives the plan: members, forecasts, simulator runs.

18
00:02:32.633 --> 00:02:41.002
The full run is up to 1,121 simulations of this 18,000-cell model, on 10 workers by default, so try one step first.

19
00:02:41.002 --> 00:02:45.732
That is the prior and one iteration first: about 200 simulations.

20
00:02:45.732 --> 00:03:02.301
We cut the wait; its real length is in the description.

21
00:03:02.301 --> 00:03:19.837
The report gives the data mismatch and figures.

22
00:03:19.837 --> 00:03:30.902
Here is one of those figures.

23
00:03:30.902 --> 00:03:33.071
Without a deck, describe the case.

24
00:03:33.071 --> 00:03:42.068
The converter then draws a true model from the prior, asks before running the truth once on OPM Flow, and writes its observations with noise.

25
00:03:42.068 --> 00:03:59.244
Here we answer no, and run the truth in the next scene.

26
00:03:59.244 --> 00:04:04.157
If you answered no to the truth run (or gave --no-observe), run it later.

27
00:04:04.157 --> 00:04:20.784
A created case is synthetic: it has a true model, so its runs also report how close the members come to it (RMSE).

28
00:04:20.784 --> 00:04:22.288
Now in the web UI.

29
00:04:22.288 --> 00:04:27.048
From Examples we copy 3dBox: production data on OPM Flow.

30
00:04:27.048 --> 00:04:41.594
Each copies the whole example, BOX.DATA and observations.csv included.

31
00:04:41.594 --> 00:04:48.874
Open Agents, and under Bring a case give the deck and its history by their workspace paths, as the Editor lists them.

32
00:04:48.874 --> 00:04:50.057
We import the deck.

33
00:04:50.057 --> 00:04:51.301
The case is a draft.

34
00:04:51.301 --> 00:05:10.295
Open its config.toml in the Editor to review, validate and submit it.

35
00:05:10.295 --> 00:05:15.795
Thank you for watching.
